<% prose = "mt-3 max-w-2xl text-base leading-7 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400" %>
<% step = "mt-12 text-lg font-semibold" %>

<p class="<%= prose %>">
  The gem ships no compiled stylesheet. Every component's look is Tailwind CSS v4 source in one file,
  <code>app/assets/tailwind/unmagic_components/engine.css</code>, and your app's own Tailwind build compiles
  it with your theme. That's why Tailwind v4 is required, and why the components pick up your colours, radii
  and dark mode without any setup.
</p>

<h2 class="<%= step %>">What's in the file</h2>
<p class="<%= prose %>">
  The Ruby renders stable class names (<code>UnmagicCard</code>, <code>UnmagicInput</code>) and the file gives
  those classes their look, mostly with <code>@apply</code>:
</p>
<div class="mt-4">
  <%= render "code", id: "css_rule", language: :css, source: <<~'CSS'.strip %>
    @layer components {
      .UnmagicBadge {
        @apply inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-full bg-neutral-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium whitespace-nowrap text-neutral-600;
        @apply dark:bg-neutral-800 dark:text-neutral-400;
      }
    }
  CSS
</div>
<p class="<%= prose %>">
  Because the styles hang off the gem's own classes, Tailwind doesn't have to scan the gem's Ruby for class
  names. Tailwind always outputs rules written in a CSS layer, so every component's styles are in your build
  whether a page uses them or not.
</p>

<h2 class="<%= step %>">Importing it</h2>
<p class="<%= prose %>">
  With tailwindcss-rails, the first build writes an entry point for the file. Import it after Tailwind:
</p>
<div class="mt-4">
  <%= render "code", id: "css_import", language: :css, source: <<~'CSS'.strip %>
    /* app/assets/tailwind/application.css */
    @import "tailwindcss";
    @import "../builds/tailwind/unmagic_components";
  CSS
</div>
<p class="<%= prose %>">
  With the Tailwind CLI or an npm build, import <code>engine.css</code> by its path inside the installed gem
  (<code>bundle show unmagic-components</code>).
</p>

<h2 class="<%= step %>">Overriding a style</h2>
<p class="<%= prose %>">
  Every rule sits in <code>@layer components</code>, which comes before Tailwind's utilities. So a utility
  you pass through <code>class:</code> wins over the gem's own style, whatever its specificity:
</p>
<% utilities_source = capture do %><%%= card title: "Members", class: "rounded-none shadow-none" do %>…<%% end %><% end %>
<div class="mt-4"><%= render "code", id: "css_utilities", source: utilities_source.to_str %></div>
<p class="<%= prose %>">
  CSS you write outside a layer beats every layer, so a rule of your own for a gem class wins too. Use this
  to change a component everywhere at once:
</p>
<div class="mt-4">
  <%= render "code", id: "css_unlayered", language: :css, source: <<~'CSS'.strip %>
    .UnmagicCard {
      @apply rounded-sm shadow-none;
    }
  CSS
</div>
<p class="<%= prose %>">
  For colours, radii, fonts and dark mode, change your theme instead, and every component follows. See
  <%= link_to "Theming", theming_path, class: "underline" %> and
  <%= link_to "Colours and variables", guide_path("variables"), class: "underline" %>.
</p>
